    std::cout << smallest(2.5, 7.25) << '\n';
    std::cout << smallest<std::string>("pear", "apple") << '\n';
    std::cout << smallest<double>(2, 1.5) << '\n';
    std::cout << sumAll(points) << '\n';
    std::cout << sumAll(lengths) << '\n';
    std::cout << sumAll(parts) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A class template works the same way for a whole class. Put `template <typename T>` above it and use `T` anywhere a type goes: members, parameters, and return types.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Stack {
public:
    void push(const T& value) {
        items_.push_back(value);
    }
    // pop, top, empty, size ...
private:
    std::vector<T> items_;
};
std::cout << sizeof(Stack<int>) << '\n';  // 24, the same as a std::vector<int>
```

Nothing in `Stack<int> numbers;` has a value to deduce from, so you name the type in angle brackets. `Stack<int>` and `Stack<std::string>` are two separate classes stamped out from one template. You have been using class templates all along: `std::vector<T>`, `std::stack<T>`, and `std::map<K, V>` are class templates built the same way. This `Stack` is an adapter like `std::stack`: it wraps a container and forwards to it. A vector suits a stack, which only touches the back. A queue pops from the front, so it wraps a deque.

**Class template argument deduction** (C++17) skips the brackets when a constructor argument reveals the type: `std::vector scores = {90, 85};` is a `std::vector<int>`. Watch out: `std::vector names = {"a", "b"};` deduces `std::vector<const char*>`.

In memory, a `Stack<int>` is just its `std::vector<int>`: a 24 byte object where you declare it, with the elements on the heap. `top` or `pop` on an empty `Stack` reaches `back` or `pop_back` on an empty vector, which is undefined behavior, so check `empty()` first.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

template <typename T>
class Stack {
public:
    void push(const T& value) {
        items_.push_back(value);
    }

    void pop() {
        items_.pop_back();
    }

    const T& top() const {
        return items_.back();
    }

    bool empty() const {
        return items_.empty();
    }

    std::size_t size() const {
        return items_.size();
    }

private:
    std::vector<T> items_;
};

int main() {
    const std::vector start = {10, 20, 30};
    Stack<int> numbers;
    for (int x : start) {
        numbers.push(x);
    }
    std::cout << numbers.size() << ' ' << numbers.top() << '\n';
    numbers.pop();
    std::cout << numbers.size() << ' ' << numbers.top() << '\n';

    Stack<std::string> pages;
    pages.push("home");
    pages.push("about");
    while (!pages.empty()) {
        std::cout << pages.top() << '\n';
        pages.pop();
    }
    std::cout << pages.size() << '\n';
    return 0;
}

