A template can take several parameters. Each `typename` adds another placeholder, deduced on its own:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}
{
    for (const auto& [key, value] : m) {
        std::cout << key << ": " << value << '\n';
    }
}

int main() {
    const std::map<std::string, int> stock = {{"pens", 12}, {"cups", 4}};
    printMap(stock);
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename K, typename V>
void printMap(const std::map<K, V>& m)
```

Passing a `std::map<std::string, int>` deduces `K = std::string` and `V = int`. Because the parameter is written as `std::map<K, V>`, this one function works for every map, whatever its key and value types.

A parameter can also be a **value** instead of a type. `std::size_t N` is a non-type template parameter: a compile-time constant baked into the type. `std::array<int, 4>` is built exactly this way, so a template can deduce both the element type and the length:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}
{
    T total{};
    for (const T& x : a) {
        total += x;
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    const std::array<double, 3> weights = {1.5, 2.25, 0.75};
    std::cout << sumArray(weights) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename T, std::size_t N>
T sumArray(const std::array<T, N>& a)
```

A `std::array<double, 3>` argument sets `T = double` and `N = 3`, and inside the body `N` is a constant like any other. `std::array<int, 3>` and `std::array<int, 4>` are different types, so each length gets its own instantiation. You can also fix the element type and template only the length: `template <std::size_t N> void zeroAll(std::array<int, N>& a)`.

Pass containers by `const&` as usual. A template changes which types fit, not the cost of a copy.

example.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

template <typename K, typename V>
void printMap(const std::map<K, V>& m) {
    for (const auto& [key, value] : m) {
        std::cout << key << ": " << value << '\n';
    }
}

template <typename T, std::size_t N>
T sumArray(const std::array<T, N>& a) {
    T total{};
    for (const T& x : a) {
        total += x;
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    const std::map<std::string, int> stock = {{"pens", 12}, {"cups", 4}};
    const std::map<int, double> prices = {{2, 1.5}, {1, 0.25}};
    printMap(stock);
    printMap(prices);

    const std::array<int, 4> steps = {3, 5, 7, 9};
    const std::array<double, 3> weights = {1.5, 2.25, 0.75};
    std::cout << sumArray(steps) << '\n';
    std::cout << sumArray(weights) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

