Sometimes one template needs different code for different kinds of types. `#include <type_traits>` gives you compile-time questions about a type:

- `std::is_integral_v<T>` is true for `int`, `long long`, `char`, and `bool`.
- `std::is_floating_point_v<T>` is true for `double` and `float`.

`if constexpr (condition)` checks such a constant while compiling and keeps only the branch that applies. The other branches are **discarded**: they are never compiled for that `T`, so they may use operations `T` does not support. This only works inside a template.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

template <typename T>
int lengthOf(const T& value) {
    {{code}}
    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
        int digits = 1;
        T rest = value;
        while (rest >= 10) {
            rest /= 10;
            ++digits;
        }
        return digits;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << lengthOf(12345) << '\n';
    std::cout << lengthOf(std::string("hello")) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
    // count the digits of value
} else {
    return static_cast<int>(value.size());  // never compiled for int
}
```

With a plain `if`, both branches compile for every `T`, and `lengthOf(12345)` fails with `request for member 'size' in 'value', which is of non-class type 'const int'`. Nothing is checked at run time, so the stamped-out `int` copy holds only the digit loop.

> Gotcha: `char` and `bool` count as integral, so `kindOf('x')` says integer and `kindOf(true)` does too. Test `std::is_same_v<T, bool>` first when they need their own branch.

C++20 adds concepts for stating which types a template accepts. C++17 has none, so type traits and `if constexpr` are your tools.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <type_traits>
#include <vector>

template <typename T>
std::string kindOf(const T& value) {
    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
        return "integer";
    } else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v<T>) {
        return "decimal";
    } else {
        return "other";
    }
}

template <typename T>
int lengthOf(const T& value) {
    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
        // counts the digits of a non-negative value
        int digits = 1;
        T rest = value;
        while (rest >= 10) {
            rest /= 10;
            ++digits;
        }
        return digits;
    } else {
        return static_cast<int>(value.size());
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << kindOf(42) << ' ' << kindOf(2.5) << ' ' << kindOf(std::string("hi")) << ' ' << kindOf('x') << '\n';
    const std::vector<int> scores = {7, 8, 9};
    std::cout << lengthOf(12345) << '\n';
    std::cout << lengthOf(std::string("hello")) << '\n';
    std::cout << lengthOf(scores) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

